 shiny buildings it's the built  environment we humans are meant to  inhabit a building on its own is just a  heap of bricks metal and concrete its  intended purpose the kind of people it  will serve its location accessibility  those are what make a building worth  talking about but very often these  quote-unquote urbanism channels only  focus on the physical object and some  easy remarkable numbers nothing more  sure the new administrative capital  looks impressive lots of big buildings  big big buildings but that alone tells  us nothing about what it really is a  dictator's [ __ ] penalty project at  the expense of tens of millions were  forced to live in poverty for decades to  come but it didn't have to be this way  egyptian authorities could have put  their traffic management in order create  a workable municipal bus system build  new metro lines more affordable housing  and they could have improved cairo's  chaotic urban planning instead they  chose to build a 40 billion dollar  monument to hubris and sociopathy  brainlessly celebrated by people who  ought to know better welcome to egypt's  new administrative capital where  buildings are big and brains are smooth  following my desert megaproject  tradition i'll leave you with a poem  this one is titled ozimendis by perseve  i met a traveler from an antique land  who said do vast and trunkless legs of  stone stand in the desert near them on  the sand half sonky shattered visage  lies whose frown and wrinkled lip and  sneer of cold command tell that its  sculptor well those passions read which  yet survive stemmed on these lifeless  things the hand that mocked them and the  heart that fed and on the pedestal these  words appear my name is ozymendis king  of kings look on my works ye mighty and  despair nothing beside remains from the  decay of that colossal wreck boundless  and bare the lowland level sands stretch  far away
 

